/// imported
decision discovery-sanctions: "ALJ Rules on Discovery Sanctions" by examiner cite cfr210.33.b
{
  group discovery-group
  note "Non-monetary sanctions include an adverse inference, treating disputed matters as resolved against the non-compliant party, evidence preclusion, striking filings, and 'other sanctions under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 37(b)' — one of the few places Part 210 explicitly imports an FRCP provision by name. Monetary sanctions (attorney's fees) run under 210.33(c), except never against the United States, the Commission, or Commission investigative attorneys. Case-dispositive default authored at 0.15 — APPROXIMATE but grounded: Certain Opaque Polymers (337-TA-883) is the teaching case for this exact branch (Chief ALJ Pender: 'spoliation on a staggering scale'; Federal Circuit affirmed both the default sanction and the resulting 25-year exclusion order)."
  _ "Non-case-dispositive sanction imposed — adverse inference, evidence preclusion, or fee-shift (Rule 210.33(b)-(c)); discovery continues" -> discovery-open p 0.85 {
    authority cite cfr210.33.b
  }
  _ "Case-dispositive default sanction for egregious noncompliance (Rule 210.33(b)) — cf. Organik Kimya spoliation, 337-TA-883" -> default-found p 0.15 {
    authority cite cfr210.33.b
    valence bad
    court_listener_url "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4348900/organik-kimya-san-ve-tic-as-v-international-trade-commission/"
  }
}

